#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "2ba74c8a-f3b2-5a1d-a596-9f41be61e7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7493" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n1. The number of calls received at a telephone center over a 10-minute period\n2. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n3. The number of pairs of shoes produced with defects in a factory in a single day" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (10 minutes).\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. The pairs of shoes produced with defects can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one day).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8ebf979-62b1-53f6-bb77-685b22e25190" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable at Some Value"}] :question/id #uuid "0f9af894-7d9c-5535-8db7-ff928a5a290a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7495" :question/problem "In a telephone exchange, an average of $3$ prank calls are received per day. What is the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls tomorrow? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that prank calls occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of prank calls received by the telephone exchange. Since the average rate is $3$ prank calls per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of prank calls can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 3\n$$\n\nand we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$.\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of receiving $5$ prank calls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 5) &= f(5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{5}e^{-3}}{5!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.101\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "ae98c2fc-bfb5-5f5d-bfce-f536aa42e5d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7496" :question/problem "On average, $4$ traffic accidents occur per week on a particular freeway. What is the probability of fewer than $3$ accidents next week? You may assume that accidents occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of traffic accidents. Since the average rate is $4$ traffic accidents per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of traffic accidents can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 4\n$$\n\nand we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$.\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $3$ traffic accidents on the next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 3) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4^{0}e^{-4}}{0!} + \\frac{4^{1}e^{-4}}{1!} + \\frac{4^{2}e^{-4}}{2!} \\\\\n&= e^{-4}(\\frac{4^{0}}{0!} + \\frac{4^{1}}{1!} + \\frac{4^{2}}{2!}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.238\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "5be23338-73b5-50b5-8dc8-0aa6d264afb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7497" :question/problem "In a factory, $3$ machines, on average, suffer some fault during the course of a week. What is the probability that at least $2$ machines will register a fault next week? You may assume that faults occur independently and at a constant average rate." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of machines with a fault in the factory. Since the average rate is $3$ faulty machines per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of machines with faults can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 3\n$$\n\nand we can write $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$.\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2)\n$$\n\nwe will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = 1 - P(X< 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 2) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1}) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{0}e^{-3}}{0!} + \\frac{3^{1}e^{-3}}{1!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.199\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of at least $2$ machines registering a fault next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= 1 - P(X < 2) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.199 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.801\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73b026df-b888-5e60-b448-c239cd3e05cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d42e553f-3e9d-58be-8873-e194aca926bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156082} {:answer/id #uuid "ea18db5f-0171-59a6-9f31-520d93917b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156083} {:answer/id #uuid "19783858-dfe7-58cd-abef-2dc018f34baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156084} {:answer/id #uuid "602d7b77-90db-52f2-9168-d97f0886bdc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156085} {:answer/id #uuid "6607e582-e426-5f0e-8a65-3995ad6fad13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.333" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156086}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c33db247-94fb-5ccc-9b40-da92a225e993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156081}] :question/id #uuid "41714e88-969e-5fd4-a843-bf3505bddd1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85251" :question/problem "A writer is editing the first draft of a book. On average, there have been $4$ typos per page. What is the probability of finding fewer than $4$ typos on the next page? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typos occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ffe541-9864-5d3f-92d0-8cc32d05b44f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Poisson Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f398f3-511f-5cf6-ae1e-168b09baf4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156094} {:answer/id #uuid "b12c04b4-ad2c-569e-b24f-0043d6194cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156095} {:answer/id #uuid "b3228ccf-2656-55d9-9eb9-6e7d2ffe101a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156096} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc836ed-053c-5a8d-a101-3f2f433a8018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156097} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c7737e-d54b-52cb-85ec-26caa774879e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156098}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f4b2db-c964-5c7c-a6cc-026433669cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156093}] :question/id #u